  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/a1/Tales_to_Astonish_Vol_1_49.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Hank accidentally misjudges the amount of enlarging fluid to take and grows to mammoth size, busting through his house (in New Jersey we learn) --scaring the gardener.&nbsp; Thankfully, Jan is nearby to give him a reducing pellet, and soon, he's back to his normal size, but he tells her in the next moment that he's learned an important lesson, which is that he mustn't grow larger than 12 feet, or his muscles won't be able to support his own weight.\n\nJan is happy with his size now (and repeatedly tells him so, flirting every chance she gets).&nbsp; But Hank is blinded by his analysis of the situation, pointing out that he's morphed both the \"growth and shrinking vapors into capsule form\" and even designed a new uniform that is more suitable to being \"larger than life size.\"&nbsp; The only job now is to perfect them.&nbsp; He deflects Jan's every comment about her falling in love with him, even to the point of calling her \"kid\" and saying, \"Honey, you're just in love with the idea of <i>being</i> in love!\"&nbsp; to which Jan replies \"Even when you call me 'honey' you make it sound...<i>medicinal</i>!\"\n\nAfter hours of practice, he finally develops a capsule that grows him to exactly 12 feet, with Jan commenting about how she feels like the assistant to a mad scientist.\n\nMeanwhile, at a local park, the reader is introduced to a strange character the New Jersey police are trying to track down because of reports of him <i>erasing</i> people.&nbsp; An unfortunate hot dog salesman wanders too close to the figure and finds himself being erased, simply with the wave of his hands.\n\nWe soon learn that he's targeting <i>specific </i>people, namely \"atomic experts,\" and that he's already erased three of the five names on his list.&nbsp; In moments, he tracks down a fourth - a Professor Ebbhart -- who also finds himself erased from existence with the wave of the stranger's hands.&nbsp; The last name on his list is none other than \"Henry Pym, the experimental wizard,\" and the stranger turns to track him down.\n\nIn the home of Hank Pym, he and Jan continue to review the differently-colored size-changing capsules, as he deems it critical that they both have this firmly committed to memory.&nbsp; Each one \"corresponds to a different reaction in growth or shrinkage.\"&nbsp; Being able to have immediate access to the correct one is so important that he's designed a belt for each of them to wear that ejects capsules at the touch of a button.&nbsp; It's not a flattering fashion choice (as Jan points out), but Hank claims it \"may save our lives someday!\"\n\nBut even as Hank directs Jan to test the new ejection mechanism, the green-skinned stranger intrudes, and with a wave of his hands, he begins to erase Hank from existence!&nbsp; However, Hank soon discovers that the man <i>actually</i> has merely <i>transported</i> him to some other place.&nbsp; He is soon joined by the \"Eraser\" himself, and it turns out he's travelled to an alternate dimension (described as \"Dimension Z\"), one that apparently occupies the same physical space as his.\n\nThe ruler of the land (referred to as \"Supremacy\") then explains that they've been aware of Hank's home for \"ages,\" only being mildly amused by what they refer to as primitives, and only after mankind developed atomic power did they feel compelled to step in and interfere, developing the \"erasing\" technology as a means of transporting themselves between the universes, all with the goal of collecting atomic scientists that can provide <i>them</i> with weapons like the ones the humans already have.\n\nBelieving that they only want the technology as a means to invade Earth, Hank tries to fight back, but he is subdued quickly.&nbsp; Only after being bound does he discover that the Wasp was transported to this realm as well, having been resting on Hank's finger at the point of transport by the Eraser.&nbsp; He requests that she give him a reducing capsule so that he can slip out of his bonds, and after a small bit of rummaging around with the options on her belt, Jan finally does so - giving him one that will reduce him to a mere one foot tall.&nbsp; It works, though Hank is annoyed with her banter, thinking to himself, \"<i>One billion</i> females in the world, and I had to pick that exquisite empty-head for a partner!\"\n\nHank's small size allows him to slip through the beings from Dimension Z, while at the same time, the Wasp also provides cover, giving Hank time to finally prepare his own uniform.&nbsp; Before long, he's joined the Wasp at ant size, and the two begin searching for the other scientists, finally discovering them in a prison.&nbsp; The beings from Dimension Z are keeping them alive for now, at least until they teach the beings there \"how to tame the atom\" and only <i>then</i> will they dispose of them.\n\nWorking together, Hank and Jan disable the prison room's scanning device and let the scientists know they're present and trying to find a way back to Earth.&nbsp; But soon, the inhabitants detect that the signal to the room has been cut and rush to see what's happened.&nbsp; However, when they arrive, they find... <i>Giant Man</i> waiting for them!\n\nDespite the aliens' best efforts, the giant sized intruder proves to be too much.&nbsp; Giant-Man is able to evade or overpower everything they throw at him, giving Jan time to quietly locate the \"chief scientist of this whole dimension.\"\n\nThey soon find that only the hand units that the so-called Eraser wears can transport them home, and soon, Giant-Man and the Wasp steal them from him, return to the prisoners so that they can be sent home -- and then finally return themselves.",
  "grade_one_liner": "A mash‑up of earlier dimensional invasions and scientist snatchings, with the erasing trick as the lone spark.&nbsp; You finish it out of obligation, <i>not</i> engagement.",
  "subtitle": "Erased!&nbsp; But Not My Deja Vu...",
  "review_text": "The plot is dull and reeks of repetition, as we find more aliens watching Earth (actually beings from another dimension rather than actual aliens).\n\nNot even one year earlier (September of last year), [LINK:1962:ST_103_torch0|ST #103] (<i>Strange Tales</i>) featured the Human Torch being taken to the \"5<sup>th</sup> Dimension\" because the ruler there said, \"Ever since we discovered your world co-existing beside ours, we have prepared to conquer it!\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=(That sure sounds a <i>lot</i> like the old \"alien invasion\" trope and very similar to dialogue in <i>these</i> pages.)]\n\nGranted, the passageway between dimensions <i>that time</i> was in a swamp, whereas <i>this</i> one involves an interesting transport mechanism that makes it <i>appear</i> as if the target is being erased.&nbsp; And while the Supremacy of this \"Dimension Z\" never actually <i>states</i> that the reason they want atomic weapons is for the purposes of conquest, that is still what Hank concludes after hearing their backstory, so as far as I'm concerned, it might as well be the same plot, just with a different transport mechanism.\n\nNot only that, but October's [LINK:1962:JIM_87_thor0|JIM #87] (<i>Journey Into Mystery</i>) <i>also</i> featured a plot involving kidnapped scientists (with Dr. Don Blake being <i>one</i> of them), and December's [LINK:1962:TTA_41_ant0|TTA #41] (this very <i>magazine</i>) showed Ant-Man battling against Kulla the Tyrant \"in another dimension of space and time\" who captured scientists to build him \"an electro-death ray.\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Come on, Stan!&nbsp; Stop telling the same story!]\n\nTaken together, these examples form a clear pattern.&nbsp; This plot was nothing more than a warmed-over amalgamation of at least three leftover ones from 1962.&nbsp; In this case, it was <i>Dr. Henry Pym</i> that was kidnapped and hauled off to Dimension Z, along with some other scientists to provide knowledge that would allow some tyrant to develop advanced weapons.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=That <i>sure</i> smells of \"rinse and repeat\" to me!]\n\n[ASSET: left | MeetTheEraser | width=11%] For the record, I'll happily grant that the visual \"erasing\" effect was [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=quite nice], and even a bit eerie, so the story wasn't <i>completely</i> without positives.\n[BR]\n[BR] But to be clear:&nbsp; this was all an <i>artistic</i> triumph, even if the actual notion of \"erasing\" came from Stan.\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut while Stan was clearly off his game from a plot perspective, the <i>real</i> reason this issue existed was to feature the new character, namely Giant-Man.&nbsp; In fact, the entire episode seemed to exist for no other reason than to showcase Giant-Man in his very first appearance -- and I guess these new belts that allow both Hank and Jan to dispense size-shaping capsules.\n[ASSET: center | JanRummagesAround | width=67%]\n\nThat alone might be forgiven [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=if Giant-Man were worth reading about in the first place!]\n\nI may be biased in my thinking, but with both Thor and the Hulk in the Avengers, what possible use is there for a Giant-Man?&nbsp; Are we seriously being asked to believe that a man who is 12 feet tall -- or about twice as tall as a 6-foot tall human being -- will be more than about twice as strong as one, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=which is <i>hardly</i> impressive in a world with Thor and Hulk (and even Iron Man)].\n\nOn the contrary, having Ant-Man and the Wasp in the Avengers gives them a unique power set that is very distinct from the other members.&nbsp; Why is there a need for another \"strong guy\" when you've already got 2-3 powerhouses around?\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut getting back to the story, pages 10-18 were essentially a showcase reel demonstrating Giant-Man's abilities, and I mostly yawned through it.&nbsp; Very little of it mattered to the plot in the end.\n\nGiant-Man overpowered the inhabitants of Dimension Z, captured the bit of tech that could transfer them back home, rescued the scientists, and then returned with the Wasp.\n\nThe [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=day was saved], but somehow, I don't feel as if my investment in this issue was worth the time spent.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe novelty of the new character, together with the nice visual effect of characters seemingly \"erased\" from existence was enough to keep this copycat story above low-grade (though barely).\n\nIt does at least establish Hank Pym as <i>supposedly</i> some kind of powerhouse (though I'm not really buying the commensurate power boost that goes along with the added height).\n[ASSET: center | MeetGiantMan | width=67%]\n\nBut the [SPAN: severity=error; text=belt units that dispense capsules] -- <i>those</i> were a bit unnerving.&nbsp; In fact, seeing Hank and Jan popping pills, especially from a 21<sup>st</sup> century perspective, really jars you in an age where drug abuse is so widely talked about.&nbsp; The days of an \"Underdog super energy pill\" was also of that same era, and was squashed in the ultra-drug-aware days of the late 70s and beyond, so it's really surprising to see it so prominently portrayed in these old stories.&nbsp; (Okay, that might have stretched [PAGE: scenario] a bit -- I <i>admit</i> it.)\n\n[ASSET: left | HandUnits | width=12%] Even so, it wasn't all bad, and I did appreciate the \"Eraser\" character, even if, in the end, it was his hand units, rather than any special power, that actually did the \"erasing.\"\n[BR]\n[BR] Be that as it may, you still have to admit:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=it <i>looked cool</i> on the printed page!]\n\n[ASSET: right | BossyHusband | width=13%] Also, Jan remained in character throughout the whole story, constantly flirting with Hank, trying to get him to pay attention to her, even complaining when he brushed aside her advances or when she thought he was being exceptionally bossy.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=Good job there, at least.]\n\nBut these moments were brief highlights in an otherwise forgettable read, and in this case, I wonder if Stan hoped readers wouldn't remember the earlier dimension stories -- but <i>he was wrong!</i>\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=BUSTED!]",
  "side_note": [
    "We learn that Hank Pym actually lives in \"a quiet house on the New Jersey Palisades overlooking the Hudson River.\""
  ],
  "decoder_ring": "The whole notion of a Giant-Man (or \"Goliath\" as he's later called) has always seemed silly, and his strength <i>way</i> overstated, though it will take another 20 or so issues of <i>Tales to Astonish </i>for Marvel to finally realize that his presence is hurting the magazine and switch instead to Sub-Mariner, who has <i>far</i> more depth to plumb (pardon the pun there) than \"Giant-Man.\"\n\nRoy Thomas, the upcoming writer on <i>Avengers</i> also seems to have a love for the character and can't seem to write the book unless there is a Giant-Man/Goliath in the group.&nbsp; I'll keep reading, but of all the four anthology-style books, this is the one I like the least and seems more of a chore to get through!&nbsp; (And given the overall quality of all of them recently, that's <i>saying</i> something!)\n\nAs for the Eraser, we haven't seen the last of him, as he appears around 13 years later in the pages of an early issue of <i>Marvel Two-In-One</i> (and I have a vague recollection of that story tying into a later pair of <i>Spectacular Spider-Man</i> issues that feature Morbius).",
  "highlight": "Giant‑Man arrives on the scene, and the Eraser tries to make sure he doesn't stay long."
}